  "why": "The statement is the full QUE conjecture for negatively curved manifolds. Unconditional proofs that Liouville is the only limit for a restricted class of eigenfunctions on a restricted class of (arithmetic, negatively curved, finite-volume) manifolds count as `case`: Lindenstrauss, Holowinsky–Soundararajan, Brooks–Lindenstrauss, Shem-Tov–Silberman 2022 (its 2025 v2 shows no new mathematics, so it stays a 2022 event) and the March 2026 Pitale-lift theorem, which is the only post-snapshot case and is an unreviewed preprint. The entropy lower bounds are `bound`s on a quantity. Full-support, limit-structure and non-escape results (Dyatlov–Jin, Dyatlov–Jin–Nonnenmacher, Kim–Miller, Shem-Tov–Silberman H⁴, de Faveri–Shem-Tov 2024) constrain the limits without identifying them, so they are `none`: steps toward a proof, not a settled restriction. Conditional results (Bisain et al., Nelson 2025) reduce QUE to unproven subconvexity, so they are `none`. Different objects or settings are `variant`: billiards, holomorphic forms, shrinking sets, averaged thin sets, Eisenstein series, spheres and cat maps. No event credits any AI system.",
  "doubts": "Treating arithmetic Hecke QUE as a `case` rather than a `variant` is a judgement call, because the conjecture concerns every eigenbasis. It is kept as `case` because the Hecke eigenfunctions are genuine Laplacian eigenfunctions on negatively curved manifolds, and the literature calls this the proven case. The Pitale-lift paper has no reviewer engagement, and the record does not say what changed in its v3. The Brooks–Lindenstrauss journal status rests on a secondary source only.",
